{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794781263668,"sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeZfELy9ETJ0Z0Sg","part":{"partID":"prt_1e14ca823001VwTxBDTN70sN16","sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeZfELy9ETJ0Z0Sg","messageID":"msg_1e14c7321001EpiRxpkxDr3FJP","type":"tool","id":"call_1eeff86ef804464990ec60de","tool":"edit","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"newString":"- Diffing `git ls-tree` at the fix commit against both README trees proved section 4 complete and section 3 one folder short without a second directory scan (iteration 1).\n- Reading the launcher allowlist and the test scope together proved the ten-name fixture cannot leak a switch into the daemon child env and cannot be bent by a developer's flags file (iteration 2).\n- Counting the three prompt files with `wc -l` kept the holdout rule while giving the report a real row count (iteration 2).\n\n---\n\n## 7. WHAT FAILED\n- Reading `ENV-REFERENCE.md:74-75` alone made the alias fix look complete; the canonical index its own header defers to still disagreed (iteration 1).\n- Treating the pass-five verifier's fix column as the full scope of the confirmed finding hid the second site the pass-five iteration had already named (iteration 1).\n- Counting the alias list as a finding in both iterations would have doubled one defect; the duplication had to be recorded as the cost on the one incomplete copy (iteration 2).\n- The per-iteration tool budget was exceeded while reading the trust boundary; the count is recorded rather than trimmed to look compliant (iteration 2).","oldString":"- Diffing `git ls-tree` at the fix commit against both README trees proved section 4 complete and section 3 one folder short without a second directory scan (iteration 1).\n\n---\n\n## 7. WHAT FAILED\n- Reading `ENV-REFERENCE.md:74-75` alone made the alias fix look complete; the canonical index its own header defers to still disagreed (iteration 1).\n- Treating the pass-five verifier's fix column as the full scope of the confirmed finding hid the second site the pass-five iteration had already named (iteration 1).","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/kill-switch-cli-confirm-5b/deep-review-strategy.md"},"output":"Edited specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/kill-switch-cli-confirm-5b/deep-review-strategy.md (1 replacement)","title":"edit","metadata":{"metadata":{"files":[{"file":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/kill-switch-cli-confirm-5b/deep-review-strategy.md","patch":"Index: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/kill-switch-cli-confirm-5b/deep-review-strategy.md\n===================================================================\n--- specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/kill-switch-cli-confirm-5b/deep-review-strategy.md\n+++ specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/kill-switch-cli-confirm-5b/deep-review-strategy.md\n@@ -70,14 +70,18 @@\n - Reading the fix diff beside the working tree for the five scope paths confirmed the fix range is not HEAD but none of the five files moved after it, so the citations stay valid (iteration 1).\n - Comparing the resolver's `MASTER_ALIASES` and `LEGACY_ALIASES['skill-advisor']` name for name against the two index tables found the one table the fix did not patch instead of a guessed drift (iteration 1).\n - Running a read-only probe of `hookFlagDecision` for the four `MK_` names turned \"the doc omits them\" into \"the doc omits names that observably disable the advisor\" (iteration 1).\n - Diffing `git ls-tree` at the fix commit against both README trees proved section 4 complete and section 3 one folder short without a second directory scan (iteration 1).\n+- Reading the launcher allowlist and the test scope together proved the ten-name fixture cannot leak a switch into the daemon child env and cannot be bent by a developer's flags file (iteration 2).\n+- Counting the three prompt files with `wc -l` kept the holdout rule while giving the report a real row count (iteration 2).\n \n ---\n \n ## 7. WHAT FAILED\n - Reading `ENV-REFERENCE.md:74-75` alone made the alias fix look complete; the canonical index its own header defers to still disagreed (iteration 1).\n - Treating the pass-five verifier's fix column as the full scope of the confirmed finding hid the second site the pass-five iteration had already named (iteration 1).\n+- Counting the alias list as a finding in both iterations would have doubled one defect; the duplication had to be recorded as the cost on the one incomplete copy (iteration 2).\n+- The per-iteration tool budget was exceeded while reading the trust boundary; the count is recorded rather than trimmed to look compliant (iteration 2).\n \n ---\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n","status":"modified","additions":4,"deletions":0}],"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Edited specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/kill-switch-cli-confirm-5b/deep-review-strategy.md (1 replacement)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"newString\":\"- Diffing `git ls-tree` at the fix commit against both README trees proved section 4 complete and section 3 one folder short without a second directory scan (iteration 1).\\n- Reading the launcher allowlist and the test scope together proved the ten-name fixture cannot leak a switch into the daemon child env and cannot be bent by a developer's flags file (iteration 2).\\n- Counting the three prompt files with `wc -l` kept the holdout rule while giving the report a real row count (iteration 2).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. WHAT FAILED\\n- Reading `ENV-REFERENCE.md:74-75` alone made the alias fix look complete; the canonical index its own header defers to still d